kill  and alder says  all right  i'm only a mender it's not a great power  to give up sepal says  it's your living and alder says it was  my life once  but that's gone and then supple says  well you know maybe your gift will come  back to you when what must happen has  happened i can't promise that i will try  to restore what i can of what i take  from you but we're all walking in the  night now on ground we don't know  so if i spare your dream at that price  will you thank me and alder says i will  what's the little good of my gift  against the great evil my ignorance  could do if you spare me the fear i live  in now the fear that i may do evil i'll  thank you to the end of my life so alder  is not just giving up his power  to purchase an end to dreams that are  bothering him  he's beset by a fear he thinks that he  may be the conduit for something  terrible that's about to happen to  earthsea and he wants to forestall that  so he is willing to give up his power  and they engage in a ritual that invokes  the powers of the earth at aurun a holy  site that has been unfortunately defiled  by the hovnorians  who are not particularly keen on on that  sort of thing but the pelnish wizard  knows what to do and they take away  his power  after that there's a very interesting  description  on the one hand like it says he found  himself treated and considered to be one  of the wizards he could do nothing about  it though it embarrassed him lest onyx  and supple should think he was claiming  equality with them  and it also troubled him because he was  now not even a sorcerer so on the one  hand he's he's got an elevated status  and he's sort of like you know getting  to talk shop and hang out with the  master practitioners from roke and from  from palm  on the other hand he doesn't have his  power and it's not just a loss of  that power  something else is going on  his gift was gone he had no power at all  he knew it as surely as he would have  known the loss of his sight the